response:
 To find the cross product of two vectors, we use the determinant of a matrix with the unit vectors $\mathbf{i}$, $\mathbf{j}$, and $\mathbf{k}$ in the first row, and the components of the two vectors in the next two rows. The matrix looks like this: \[
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
1 & -1 & 2 \\
3 & 4 & -5
\end{pmatrix}
\] To calculate the cross product, we compute the determinant of this matrix. The cross product is then given by the vector whose components are the result of this determinant. \[
\begin{align*}
\text{Cross product} &= \mathbf{i}((-1)(-5) - (2)(4)) - \mathbf{j}((1)(-5) - (2)(3)) + \mathbf{k}((1)(4) - (-1)(3)) \\
&= \mathbf{i}(5 - 8) - \mathbf{j}(-5 - 6) + \mathbf{k}(4 + 3) \\
&= -3\mathbf{i} + 11\mathbf{j} + 7\mathbf{k} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 11 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
\]
Answer: $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 11 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}}$

